Patch 38. In the series since 2026-10-17, after patch 37.

AllocFixed (src/core/memory_posix.cpp) maps a fixed range with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, and commits
an already reserved range on its EEXIST (mprotect). Kernels before Linux 4.17 do not know the flag
and take the address as a hint: an occupied range is mapped somewhere else and that address is
returned. The heap then believes the page at its own address committed while it is still
PROT_NONE, and the first write there faults. Android devices still ship 4.9 and 4.14 kernels
(the Galaxy Tab S5e, Android 11: 4.9.227). Now, when the result is not the address asked for, it
is unmapped and the call goes on as EEXIST. On kernels from 4.17 on the new branch is never
taken: they never return another address with that flag.

Test: heap_allocation_test.cpp, "A page allocated again after a read-only release is writable":
a page is committed, made read-only, released and allocated again at the same address, and a
write through TranslateVirtual must not fault. A release leaves the host page as its last
protection, so the new commit is the one that restores access. On the Tab S5e (4.9) it ends with
SIGSEGV without this patch, alone or in the suite, and passes with it; a reservation then
MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE at the same address returned another address there (Linux 7.0: EEXIST).
With the patch the tablet passes the whole suite. On Linux, ctest as before.

diff --git a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
index ba533ec..86190c9 100644
--- a/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
+++ b/src/core/memory_posix.cpp
@@ -294,6 +294,16 @@ void* AllocFixed(void* base_address, size_t length, AllocationType allocation_ty
 #endif
 
   void* result = mmap(base_address, length, prot_initial, flags, -1, 0);
+#if defined(MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE) && REX_PLATFORM_LINUX
+  // Kernels before Linux 4.17 (Android devices still ship 4.9 and 4.14) do not know
+  // MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE and take the address as a hint: an occupied range is mapped
+  // somewhere else instead of failing with EEXIST.
+  if (result != MAP_FAILED && base_address && result != base_address) {
+    munmap(result, length);
+    result = MAP_FAILED;
+    errno = EEXIST;
+  }
+#endif
   if (result != MAP_FAILED) {
     return result;
   }
diff --git a/tests/unit/memory/heap_allocation_test.cpp b/tests/unit/memory/heap_allocation_test.cpp
index 904290d..308887d 100644
--- a/tests/unit/memory/heap_allocation_test.cpp
+++ b/tests/unit/memory/heap_allocation_test.cpp
@@ -257,6 +257,30 @@ TEST_CASE("AllocFixed commit on reserved region succeeds", "[memory][heap]") {
   heap->Release(target, nullptr);
 }
 
+TEST_CASE("A page allocated again after a read-only release is writable", "[memory][heap]") {
+  // A release leaves the host page as its last protection (read-only here), so the next commit
+  // of that page has to restore access on a mapping that is already there. Kernels before Linux
+  // 4.17 took MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE as a hint and mapped that commit elsewhere instead: the write
+  // below faulted (patch 38).
+  auto& memory = GetTestMemory();
+  auto* heap = MutableHeap(memory.LookupHeap(0x10000000));
+  REQUIRE(heap != nullptr);
+
+  const uint32_t target = 0x21200000;
+  const uint32_t reserve_commit =
+      rex::memory::kMemoryAllocationReserve | rex::memory::kMemoryAllocationCommit;
+  const uint32_t read_write = rex::memory::kMemoryProtectRead | rex::memory::kMemoryProtectWrite;
+  REQUIRE(heap->AllocFixed(target, 4096, 4096, reserve_commit, read_write));
+  REQUIRE(heap->Protect(target, 4096, rex::memory::kMemoryProtectRead, nullptr));
+  REQUIRE(heap->Release(target, nullptr));
+  REQUIRE(heap->AllocFixed(target, 4096, 4096, reserve_commit, read_write));
+  auto* host = memory.TranslateVirtual<volatile uint8_t*>(target);
+  host[0] = 0x5A;
+  CHECK(host[0] == 0x5A);
+
+  heap->Release(target, nullptr);
+}
+
